WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:05.549
Despite being a company which prides itself
on making kids' movies, Disney is responsible

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:08.560
for some of the most disturbing death scenes
in cinema history.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.780
From tragic family loss to straight-up body
horror, these are the Disney deaths that traumatized

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.600
us as children.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.740
Considering so many Disney characters tend
to be orphans, The Lion King’s Simba could

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.210
be counted as lucky, this little lion cub
has got a loving mom and his father Mufasa

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.300
is protective, gentle and understanding.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.760
Until he gets trampled to death, that is.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.160
In the film's darkest moment, Simba finds
himself trapped in a gorge, desperately trying

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.460
to outrun a herd of stampeding wildebeest.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.820
Just when it looks like he’s going to wind
up as a Pride Land pancake, Mufasa comes to

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.430
the rescue, leaping into the horde of hooves
and horns to save his kid.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.560
But while Mufasa gets Simba to safety, climbing
out of the gorge proves to be too difficult

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.540
for the mighty king.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.440
Fortunately, his dear old brother Scar is
there to help.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.200
“Long live the king.”

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.620
While the film makes clear that Mufasa has
never been on great terms with his brother,

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.880
there’s still a look of genuine betrayal
on his face when Scar tosses him down to his

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:05.380
doom.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.240
But the real heartache comes when Simba tries
to get his dad to wake up, nuzzling his lifeless

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.260
body before finally relenting and heading
into exile.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.460
Back in 1994, between the 1967 animated classic
and the 2016 remake, Disney released a live-action

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.560
version of The Jungle Book.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.691
The plot of the film has Mowgli forced to
lead a band of British soldiers to a stash

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.620
of hidden treasure.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.400
But as Mowgli and his sadistic captors head
into the jungle, this PG kids' flick slowly

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.860
transforms into a genuine horror movie.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.600
One villain falls from a cliff, another is
mauled by a tiger, while the big bad eventually

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.020
ends up in a watery grave, before being finished
off by a gigantic python.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:48.200
At one point, a British bully falls into a
pit of quicksand, and, despite his friends'

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.660
best efforts, slowly sinks into the muck,
crying in terror all the way down.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.360
Another baddie finds himself caught in a temple
tramp, left helpless as the roof seals shut

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.480
and the room fills with sand, entombing him
for all eternity.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.940
Not exactly the bare necessities, is it?

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.980
Despite the occasional moment of levity, The
Hunchback of Notre Dame isn’t afraid to

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:08.500
get dark.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.620
With that said, it should come as no surprise
that when the villainous Claude Frollo meets

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.620
his end, it comes in the most disturbing way
possible.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.640
In the film's big finale, Frollo is chasing
the hunchback Quasimodo and his love Esmeralda

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.520
through the halls of Notre Dame, hoping to
catch and kill the object of his desire.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.360
The chase eventually leads to the cathedral's
balcony, and after a brief showdown, this

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:29.860
happens:

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.200
Even kids can probably understand the hellish
metaphor on display here, and while Frollo

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.610
does deserve his fate, watching him fall into
the flames is still enough to make anyone

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:48.980
fear for their immortal soul.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.300
The folks at Disney know there's nothing scarier
for a kid than the idea that their mom could

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:54.800
die.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.680
That’s one of the reasons why Bambi is basically
the saddest animated movie ever made, and,

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.130
over sixty years later, Pixar followed in
Bambi's hoof prints by traumatizing a whole

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.209
new generation with Finding Nemo.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.810
The movie opens on a hopeful note, with expectant
parents Marlin and Coral moving into their

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.550
oceanfront anemone home.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.700
The two clownfish have over 400 little eggs
waiting to hatch, and they couldn't be more

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.960
excited about their future.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.260
That's when Marlin notices an approaching
barracuda, staring straight at the parents

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.750
and their unhatched kids.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.920
When the barracuda moves in for the kill,
Marlin does his best to ward off the beast,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.040
but is knocked unconscious.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.180
When he wakes up, Coral is gone, and every
egg save one has been devoured.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.900
Although we don't actually see anyone get
eaten, we do see poor Marlin mourn his dead

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.380
wife and unborn children.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:44.320
His heartbreaking sobs, coupled with the idea
you really could lose your mom at any moment,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.940
would have made so many kids feel completely
adrift while watching the movie.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.480
Davy Jones is arguably one of the great Disney
villains, thanks in large part to a CGI face

00:03:54.489 --> 00:03:58.690
that still looks amazing years after his appearance
in the Pirates of the Caribbean sequels.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.099
With his lobster hand, tentacled chin and
supernatural crew, Jones was a force to be

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.780
reckoned with, until the East India Trading
Company got hold of his heart.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.720
Stashed away in a wooden chest, the heart
gives the villainous Cutler Beckett and his

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.060
henchman Ian Mercer the ability to control
the pirate lord.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.220
Near the end of Pirates of the Caribbean:
At World's End, however, Mercer finds out

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.769
why you should never play with a buccaneer's
heart.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.840
During the film’s climactic sea battle,
a cannonball kills Mercer's men, leaving him

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.420
alone with the octopus pirate.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.240
Seizing the chance to reclaim his heart, Jones
kills Mercer in the slimiest, gruesomest way

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:31.820
possible.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.720
It honestly might be the most gruesome death
in any Disney film ever.

